window.__hvBdRegister("nicomachean-ethics", {"key":"nicomachean-ethics","sections":[{"i":0,"j":8,"loc":"Book I (1 of 5)","title":"The chief good and the method of inquiry","summary":"Aristotle opens by observing that every art, inquiry and action aims at some good, and that ends are ranked under master arts. If there is an end desired for its own sake, it is the chief good, and its study belongs to politics, the most authoritative science, whose end includes the ends of the others. He warns that ethics admits only as much precision as its variable subject allows, and that the young and those led by passion are unfit hearers, since the aim is action, not knowledge. Most people call the chief good happiness but disagree about what it is; the inquiry must start from things known to us, which requires a good upbringing.","ideas":["All activity aims at some good; subordinate ends serve master ends","Politics is the master science whose end is the human good","Precision must fit the subject; ethics speaks in outline and for the most part","Arguments must start from what is known to us, so good habits come first"],"terms":[{"t":"eudaimonia (happiness)","d":"The name almost everyone gives to the highest good achievable by action, though they disagree about its content."},{"t":"politikē (political science)","d":"The master art that orders the other sciences and aims at the good of man and of the city."}]},{"i":9,"j":14,"loc":"Book I (2 of 5)","title":"Three lives and the Form of the Good","summary":"Aristotle surveys the main candidates for happiness by the types of life: the life of enjoyment, which identifies the good with pleasure; the political life, which aims at honour or perhaps virtue; and the contemplative life, deferred for later. Honour is too superficial, depending on those who give it, and virtue alone is compatible with sleep or great misfortune; wealth is merely useful. He then examines the Platonic universal good, though its proponents are friends, since truth must be honoured above them. He argues that 'good' is said in all the categories, that there would be one science of all goods, and that an Idea of the Good would be unattainable and useless to doctors or craftsmen.","ideas":["Pleasure, honour and wealth fail as the final good","Virtue without activity is compatible with sleep and misfortune","'Good' is predicated in many categories, so there is no single Idea of it","Even a separate Good would be neither attainable nor useful for action"],"terms":[{"t":"bios theōrētikos (contemplative life)","d":"The third of the prominent lives, named here and reserved for later treatment."},{"t":"Idea of the Good","d":"The Platonic universal good, which Aristotle argues cannot be one common element in all goods."}]},{"i":15,"j":21,"loc":"Book I (3 of 5)","title":"Happiness as final, self-sufficient activity","summary":"Returning to the good sought by action, Aristotle argues that the chief good must be the most final end, chosen always for itself and never for anything else, and that happiness alone fits this description. Happiness is also self-sufficient, making life desirable and lacking in nothing, not counted as one good among others. To say more, he turns to the function of man: not nutrition or perception, shared with plants and animals, but an active life of the part that has reason. The human good is therefore activity of soul in accordance with virtue, in a complete life, for one swallow does not make a summer. This is offered as an outline, with a reminder about fitting precision and the importance of first principles.","ideas":["Happiness is the most final end, chosen for itself alone","Happiness is self-sufficient, not one good to be added to","The function argument: the human good is rational activity done well","Happiness requires a complete life"],"terms":[{"t":"ergon (function)","d":"The characteristic work of a thing, in which its good and its doing well reside."},{"t":"aretē (virtue, excellence)","d":"The quality by which a function is performed well; human good is activity in accordance with it."},{"t":"autarkeia (self-sufficiency)","d":"The mark of what, taken by itself, makes life desirable and lacking in nothing."}]},{"i":22,"j":32,"loc":"Book I (4 of 5)","title":"Testing the definition against common opinion","summary":"Aristotle checks his account of happiness against received views: goods of the soul rank highest, the happy man lives and does well, and those who equate happiness with virtue, wisdom or pleasure are partly right. What matters is activity rather than mere possession of virtue, as at Olympia the crown goes to those who compete. The virtuous life is pleasant in itself, since virtuous acts please the lover of virtue, so happiness is at once best, noblest and most pleasant. Yet it also needs external goods such as friends, wealth, birth and children. He asks whether happiness comes by learning, habit, gift of the gods or chance, and concludes it is godlike yet widely attainable by study and care, which is why animals and boys are not called happy.","ideas":["Virtuous activity, not merely possessed virtue, constitutes happiness","The good man's life has its pleasure in itself","Happiness needs external goods as instruments and conditions","Happiness is attained by training and care, not left to chance"],"terms":[{"t":"energeia (activity)","d":"Actual exercise of a capacity, contrasted with a state that may lie idle, as in sleep."}]},{"i":33,"j":48,"loc":"Book I (5 of 5)","title":"Fortune, the dead, and the parts of soul","summary":"Aristotle takes up Solon's saying that one must see the end before calling a man happy, and the puzzle of whether fortunes of descendants affect the dead. He answers that virtuous activities, the most durable human functions, determine happiness, so the good man bears fortune nobly and never becomes miserable, though disasters like Priam's can deny him blessedness; the fortunes of friends touch the dead only faintly. Happiness is something prized, like the divine, rather than praised, and Eudoxus is credited with this reasoning about pleasure. Turning to virtue, he divides the soul into a vegetative part, an appetitive part that can obey reason, and the rational part, and so distinguishes intellectual from moral virtues.","ideas":["Virtuous activity gives happiness its stability against fortune","Great misfortunes can mar but not make the good man miserable","Happiness is prized and called blessed, not praised","The soul's divisions ground intellectual and moral virtue"],"terms":[{"t":"makarios (blessed)","d":"The fullest happiness, which grave misfortunes like Priam's can take away."},{"t":"the appetitive element","d":"The irrational part that shares in reason by listening to and obeying it, as one heeds a father."},{"t":"ēthikē vs dianoētikē aretē","d":"Moral virtues such as temperance and liberality versus intellectual virtues such as wisdom and understanding."}]},{"i":49,"j":68,"loc":"Book II (1 of 3)","title":"Moral virtue comes from habituation","summary":"Aristotle explains that intellectual virtue comes mostly from teaching, but moral virtue from habit, so it is neither by nature nor contrary to nature: we are adapted to receive it and perfected by practice. We become just by doing just acts, as builders by building, and the same activities that produce a virtue can destroy it, so habits formed from youth make all the difference. Virtues are destroyed by excess and defect and preserved by the mean, and they concern pleasures and pains, which are the sign of a formed character. To the puzzle of how one becomes just by doing just acts, he answers that a virtuous act requires knowledge, choice for its own sake, and a firm character; theory without practice heals the soul no more than listening to doctors heals the body.","ideas":["Moral virtue is formed by repeated action, not given by nature","Excess and defect destroy virtue; the mean preserves it","Pleasure and pain in acting are signs of character","A virtuous act needs knowledge, choice for its own sake, and stable character"],"terms":[{"t":"ethos / ēthikē (habit / moral)","d":"Moral virtue takes its name from habit, from which it arises."},{"t":"hexis (state of character)","d":"The settled condition produced by like activities."}]},{"i":69,"j":80,"loc":"Book II (2 of 3)","title":"Virtue defined as a mean","summary":"Asking what virtue is, Aristotle sorts things in the soul into passions, faculties and states of character, and shows virtue is a state, since we are not praised or blamed for passions or capacities. Every excellence makes its possessor good and its work done well. In what is continuous one can take more, less or the equal, and the right amount relative to us differs from the arithmetical mean, as a trainer would not give six pounds to Milo and a beginner alike. Moral virtue aims at the intermediate in passions and actions, feeling them at the right times, toward the right objects and in the right way. He defines virtue as a state concerned with choice, lying in a mean relative to us, determined by reason as the practically wise man would determine it; some acts and feelings, like theft or envy, have no mean.","ideas":["Virtue's genus is a state of character, not a passion or faculty","The mean is relative to us, not fixed arithmetically","One can fail in many ways, succeed in only one","Some actions and passions are bad in themselves and admit no mean"],"terms":[{"t":"mesotēs (mean)","d":"The intermediate between excess and defect at which moral virtue aims."},{"t":"prohairesis (choice)","d":"Deliberate choosing; virtue is a state concerned with it."},{"t":"phronimos (man of practical wisdom)","d":"The person whose reasoning sets the standard for determining the mean."}]},{"i":81,"j":94,"loc":"Book II (3 of 3)","title":"The table of virtues and finding the mean","summary":"Aristotle applies the definition to particular cases from a table: courage, temperance, liberality, magnificence, proper pride, an unnamed virtue of ambition, good temper, truthfulness, ready wit and friendliness, plus means in feelings such as modesty and righteous indignation; justice and the intellectual virtues are postponed. He then explains that each extreme is opposed both to the mean and to the other extreme, and that one extreme is often more opposed to the mean, either because it is less like it or because we naturally tend toward it. Hitting the mean is hard, so as a second best one should steer away from the worse extreme, as Calypso advises, drag oneself away from one's own tendencies, and guard especially against pleasure. Where exactly blame begins is left to perception of particulars.","ideas":["Each virtue is set between a named or nameless excess and deficiency","The extremes are more opposed to each other than to the mean","Steer away from the greater error and from one's natural bent","The precise point of blame depends on perception of particulars"],"terms":[{"t":"megalopsuchia (proper pride)","d":"The mean concerning great honour, between empty vanity and undue humility."},{"t":"nemesis (righteous indignation)","d":"A mean in feeling, pain at undeserved good fortune, between envy and spite."}]},{"i":95,"j":105,"loc":"Book III (1 of 5)","title":"Voluntary, involuntary, and mixed actions","summary":"Since praise and blame attach to voluntary acts, Aristotle defines the involuntary as what occurs by compulsion or through ignorance. The compulsory has its moving principle outside the agent, who contributes nothing; acts done from fear, like throwing cargo overboard in a storm, are mixed but more voluntary, since chosen at the moment of action. Pleasant and noble objects do not compel. Acts done by reason of ignorance are involuntary only when they cause regret, otherwise 'not voluntary', and acting in ignorance, as when drunk, differs from acting by reason of it. The relevant ignorance is of particulars, such as what one is doing or to whom, not of the universal; acts from anger or appetite remain voluntary.","ideas":["Compulsion means the origin is outside and the agent contributes nothing","Mixed acts under duress are voluntary at the moment of action","Only ignorance of particular circumstances excuses","Acts from anger or appetite are still the agent's own"],"terms":[{"t":"hekousion / akousion (voluntary / involuntary)","d":"Voluntary acts have their origin in an agent who knows the particular circumstances."},{"t":"mixed actions","d":"Acts like jettisoning cargo, undesirable in themselves but chosen in the circumstances."}]},{"i":106,"j":118,"loc":"Book III (2 of 5)","title":"Choice and deliberation","summary":"Aristotle distinguishes choice from the broader voluntary: children and animals act voluntarily but do not choose. Choice is not appetite, anger, wish or opinion; wish can be for impossibles and concerns ends, whereas choice concerns what is in our power. It seems to be what has been decided by prior deliberation. We deliberate not about eternal things, natural regularities, chance or others' affairs, but about what we can bring about and whose outcome is uncertain. Deliberation concerns means, not ends, working back like a geometrical analysis until it reaches the first step to be taken, and choice is defined as deliberate desire of things in our power.","ideas":["Choice is narrower than the voluntary","Choice differs from appetite, anger, wish and opinion","We deliberate about means within our power, not ends","Deliberation works back from the end to the first action"],"terms":[{"t":"prohairesis (choice)","d":"Deliberate desire of things in our own power, decided upon after deliberation."},{"t":"bouleusis (deliberation)","d":"Inquiry into how an assumed end can be achieved by our own efforts."},{"t":"boulēsis (wish)","d":"Desire for the end, which may even be for impossible things."}]},{"i":119,"j":128,"loc":"Book III (3 of 5)","title":"Wish and responsibility for character","summary":"Aristotle resolves whether wish aims at the good or the apparent good: the true good is the object of wish without qualification, but each person wishes for what appears good, and the good man, judging rightly, is the norm and measure. Since virtue and vice concern means and lie in our power, both are voluntary; he rejects the saying that no one is voluntarily wicked. Legislators' punishments and rewards confirm this, as do penalties for culpable ignorance like drunkenness. People are responsible for becoming careless or unjust through their activities, even if they cannot now simply stop, as a thrown stone cannot be recalled. Against the objection that the appearance of the end is fixed by nature, he argues vice is as voluntary as virtue.","ideas":["The good man is the measure of what is truly good","Virtue and vice are equally in our power","People are responsible for the characters their actions formed","Once formed, a bad state may no longer be changed at will"],"terms":[{"t":"the apparent good","d":"What seems good to each person, which coincides with the true good only for the good man."}]},{"i":129,"j":151,"loc":"Book III (4 of 5)","title":"Courage and its counterfeits","summary":"Beginning the particular virtues, Aristotle defines courage as a mean concerning fear and confidence, chiefly regarding the noblest danger, death in battle. The brave man fears and faces the right things, for the right motive and in the right way, for the sake of the noble; the fearless madman, the rash man and the coward fall outside the mean, and dying to escape poverty or love is cowardice. He then lists five kinds of so-called courage: the citizen-soldier's, driven by honour and shame; the professional soldier's experience; passion or spirit; the sanguine confidence of past victors; and ignorance of danger. True courage concerns fear more than confidence and involves pain, and the more virtuous and happy a man is, the more painful death is to him.","ideas":["Courage is chiefly about facing death in battle for the noble","Rashness and cowardice are its excess and defect","Five imitations of courage lack its motive or knowledge","Courage involves pain and is praised for that reason"],"terms":[{"t":"andreia (courage)","d":"The mean concerning fear and confidence, facing the terrible for the sake of the noble."},{"t":"thumos (spirit, passion)","d":"The angry impulse that aids the brave and becomes courage if choice and motive are added."},{"t":"to kalon (the noble)","d":"The end for whose sake the brave man endures and acts."}]},{"i":152,"j":162,"loc":"Book III (5 of 5)","title":"Temperance and self-indulgence","summary":"Aristotle treats temperance as the mean concerning bodily pleasures, but only those of touch and taste, which humans share with animals; pleasures of sight, hearing, smell, honour or learning do not count. Natural appetites go wrong only by excess, while peculiar appetites go wrong in many ways. The self-indulgent man is pained at missing pleasures and chooses them above all else; the deficient insensible man hardly exists. The temperate man desires moderately what makes for health and other pleasures that are not ignoble or beyond his means. Self-indulgence is more voluntary than cowardice, and its name is applied to childish faults, since appetite, like a child, must be chastened and follow reason.","ideas":["Temperance concerns only touch and taste, pleasures shared with animals","Self-indulgence is excess and is pained by absence of pleasure","The temperate man desires as reason prescribes","Appetite should obey reason as a child obeys its tutor"],"terms":[{"t":"sōphrosunē (temperance)","d":"The mean regarding bodily pleasures, desiring the right things moderately and as one ought."},{"t":"akolasia (self-indulgence)","d":"Excess in bodily pleasures, literally an 'unchastened' state."}]},{"i":163,"j":178,"loc":"Book IV (1 of 3)","title":"Liberality, prodigality, and meanness","summary":"Aristotle describes liberality as the mean regarding wealth, shown above all in giving to the right people, the right amounts, at the right time, for the sake of the noble and with pleasure. The liberal man takes only from proper sources, does not neglect his property, gives relative to his means, and is seldom rich. Prodigality exceeds in giving and falls short in taking; it is curable and better than meanness, though most prodigals end up taking from wrong sources and becoming self-indulgent. Meanness, deficiency in giving and excess in taking, is incurable, innate and multiform, from cheeseparers to usurers and gamblers with a sordid love of gain. Meanness is the vice more opposed to liberality.","ideas":["Liberality lies chiefly in right giving relative to one's substance","The prodigal is foolish rather than wicked and can be cured","Meanness is incurable and takes many forms","Sordid love of gain unites the various mean types"],"terms":[{"t":"eleutheriotēs (liberality)","d":"The mean concerning the giving and taking of wealth."},{"t":"aneleutheria (meanness)","d":"Deficiency in giving and excess in taking, the worse vice opposed to liberality."}]},{"i":179,"j":195,"loc":"Book IV (2 of 3)","title":"Magnificence and proper pride","summary":"Magnificence is fitting expenditure on a large scale, like an artist's tasteful use of great sums for honour's sake, on public objects such as offerings, choruses, triremes and weddings; a poor man cannot be magnificent. Its vices are vulgar showiness and niggardliness. Aristotle then describes the proud man, who thinks himself worthy of great things and is worthy, chiefly of honour, the greatest external good. Because he deserves most, he must be good in the highest degree, so pride is a crown of the virtues. He is moderate toward fortune, faces only great dangers, gives benefits but is ashamed to receive them, is open in love and hate, and has a slow step and deep voice. Undue humility is more opposed to pride than vanity.","ideas":["Magnificence is great expenditure fitted to agent, occasion and object","The proud man claims great honour and truly deserves it","Pride presupposes all the virtues and enhances them","Undue humility is worse and commoner than vanity"],"terms":[{"t":"megaloprepeia (magnificence)","d":"Fitting expenditure on a grand scale, a larger counterpart of liberality."},{"t":"megalopsuchia (pride, greatness of soul)","d":"The state of one who rightly thinks himself worthy of great honour."},{"t":"timē (honour)","d":"The greatest of external goods and the prize of virtue."}]},{"i":196,"j":214,"loc":"Book IV (3 of 3)","title":"Ambition, temper, social virtues, and shame","summary":"Aristotle describes an unnamed mean concerning ordinary honour, between ambition and lack of ambition, which is why the extremes seem to claim the middle. Good temper is the mean concerning anger, leaning toward deficiency; the inirascible are slavish, while the irascible, choleric, sulky and bad-tempered exceed in various ways. Three social means follow: an unnamed friendliness between obsequiousness or flattery and churlishness; truthfulness about oneself between boastfulness and mock modesty, with Socrates' irony as an attractive understatement; and ready wit or tact between buffoonery and boorishness, the tactful man being a law to himself. Shame is not a virtue but a feeling suited to youth, and continence is said to be a mixed state treated later.","ideas":["Nameless means let the extremes dispute the middle place","Good temper inclines toward the deficiency; the excess is worse to live with","Truthfulness about oneself is opposed chiefly to boastfulness","Shame is a feeling becoming to youth, not a virtue"],"terms":[{"t":"praotēs (good temper)","d":"The mean regarding anger, being angry at the right things, with the right people, for the right time."},{"t":"eirōneia (mock modesty)","d":"Understating one's merits, as Socrates did; more attractive than boasting."},{"t":"eutrapelia (ready wit)","d":"Tasteful humour in leisure, between buffoonery and boorishness."}]},{"i":215,"j":225,"loc":"Book V (1 of 5)","title":"Universal and particular justice","summary":"Aristotle sets out to determine what justice concerns and what kind of mean it is. Justice and injustice are ambiguous: the unjust man is both the lawless man and the grasping, unfair man. Justice as lawfulness is complete virtue in relation to one's neighbour, since law commands the acts of every virtue; hence it is called 'another's good' and the greatest of virtues. But there is also a particular justice, a part of virtue, shown when someone acts from gain, as in adultery committed for money rather than appetite. Particular injustice concerns honour, money or safety, with gain as its motive, and is to the unfair as part to whole.","ideas":["'Unjust' means both lawless and unfair","Universal justice is complete virtue toward others","Particular justice concerns gain in honour, money or safety","Whether good man and good citizen coincide is deferred"],"terms":[{"t":"dikaiosunē (justice)","d":"The state that makes people act justly and wish what is just, in a wide and a particular sense."},{"t":"pleonexia (graspingness)","d":"Wanting more than one's share, the mark of the particularly unjust man."}]},{"i":226,"j":236,"loc":"Book V (2 of 5)","title":"Distributive and rectificatory justice","summary":"Particular justice divides into distributive justice, in the sharing of honour, money and other common goods, and rectificatory justice in transactions, both voluntary like sale and loan and involuntary like theft or assault. Distributive justice is an equality of ratios among at least four terms, two persons and two shares, awarded according to merit, though democrats, oligarchs and aristocrats define merit differently; it is geometrical proportion. Rectificatory justice ignores who the parties are and uses arithmetical proportion, with the judge, a sort of animate justice, restoring the equal by taking from gain and adding to loss. The just here is an intermediate between a kind of gain and loss.","ideas":["Distribution follows merit by geometrical proportion","Rectification treats the parties as equal and uses arithmetical proportion","The judge restores equality like halving a divided line","'Gain' and 'loss' come from the language of voluntary exchange"],"terms":[{"t":"distributive justice","d":"Allocation of common goods in proportion to the worth of the recipients."},{"t":"rectificatory justice","d":"Correction of transactions by restoring the equal between gain and loss."},{"t":"dikastēs (judge)","d":"The mediator who seeks the intermediate, described as justice embodied."}]},{"i":237,"j":245,"loc":"Book V (3 of 5)","title":"Reciprocity, exchange, and money","summary":"Aristotle rejects the Pythagorean view that justice is simple reciprocity, since it fits neither distributive nor rectificatory justice. In exchange, however, proportionate reciprocity holds the city together, effected by cross-conjunction between, for example, a builder and a shoemaker whose products must be equated. Money was introduced as a measure making unlike goods commensurate; the true unit is demand, which money represents by convention, hence its name from law. Money also serves as surety for future exchange and is steadier in value than goods. He concludes that justice is a mean in that it relates to an intermediate amount, between doing and suffering injustice.","ideas":["Simple retaliation is not justice","Proportionate requital in exchange holds the city together","Money measures goods by representing demand, by convention","Just action lies between having too much and too little"],"terms":[{"t":"antipeponthos (reciprocity)","d":"Return for what one has done or received, just only when proportionate."},{"t":"nomisma (money)","d":"A conventional measure of demand, named from nomos because it exists by law, not nature."},{"t":"chreia (demand, need)","d":"The real unit that holds exchange together."}]},{"i":246,"j":258,"loc":"Book V (4 of 5)","title":"Political justice and degrees of wrongdoing","summary":"Aristotle notes that doing an unjust act does not make one unjust. Political justice exists among free and equal people living under law; justice toward slaves and children is so only by analogy, while household justice toward a wife comes closer. Political justice is partly natural, partly legal, and even natural justice is changeable among humans, though the right hand is still stronger by nature. Injustice requires voluntariness, so he grades injuries: misadventures contrary to expectation, mistakes, acts of injustice done knowingly in passion without deliberation, and acts done by choice, which alone make a man unjust. Some involuntary acts are excusable and some are not.","ideas":["Political justice holds among free equals under law","Justice is partly natural and partly conventional, yet all changeable","Misadventure, mistake, passionate wrong and chosen wrong differ","Only wrongs done from choice make the agent unjust"],"terms":[{"t":"politikon dikaion (political justice)","d":"Justice among those who share a life under law with a view to self-sufficiency."},{"t":"phusikon / nomikon dikaion (natural / legal justice)","d":"What has the same force everywhere versus what is indifferent until laid down."}]},{"i":259,"j":276,"loc":"Book V (5 of 5)","title":"Puzzles of injustice and equity","summary":"Aristotle asks whether one can be willingly treated unjustly, and answers no, since injustice is contrary to the wish of the sufferer; Glaucus giving gold armour for bronze is not wronged. The distributor, not the recipient of an excess share, acts unjustly. Being just is not easy, because it requires acting from a certain state, and justice is essentially human. Equity is a correction of legal justice where the law, being universal, fails a particular case, like the flexible leaden rule of Lesbian builders; the equitable man takes less than his legal due. A man cannot treat himself unjustly except in a metaphorical sense between parts of the soul, and suicide wrongs the state. The book closes the account of moral virtues.","ideas":["No one is voluntarily treated unjustly","The distributor, not the receiver, does the wrong","Equity corrects the law where its universality errs","Doing injustice is worse than suffering it"],"terms":[{"t":"epieikeia (equity)","d":"The just that corrects the law where it is defective owing to its universality."},{"t":"Lesbian rule","d":"A leaden builder's rule that bends to the stone, an image for decrees fitted to facts."}]},{"i":277,"j":289,"loc":"Book VI (1 of 3)","title":"The right rule and the intellectual states","summary":"Aristotle notes that saying one should choose the mean as the right rule dictates is true but not clear, so the right rule and its standard must be defined. He divides the rational part of the soul into a scientific part, which studies invariable things, and a calculative part, which deliberates about variable things. Since choice is deliberate desire, good choice needs true reasoning and right desire, and the work of both intellectual parts is truth. He lists five states by which the soul grasps truth: art, scientific knowledge, practical wisdom, philosophic wisdom and intuitive reason, then defines scientific knowledge as a capacity to demonstrate about the necessary, and art as a reasoned state concerned with making.","ideas":["Choice is desiderative reason or ratiocinative desire","Scientific and calculative parts of the soul answer to invariable and variable objects","Five states that reach truth; opinion and judgement are excluded because they can err","Making and acting differ, so art is not action"],"terms":[{"t":"orthos logos (right rule)","d":"The rational standard that fixes the mean in each state of character."},{"t":"epistēmē (scientific knowledge)","d":"A state of capacity to demonstrate about what is necessary and eternal."},{"t":"technē (art)","d":"A state concerned with making, involving a true course of reasoning."}]},{"i":290,"j":306,"loc":"Book VI (2 of 3)","title":"Practical wisdom, intuitive reason and philosophic wisdom","summary":"Practical wisdom is identified from the people credited with it, such as Pericles: it is a true, reasoned state of capacity to act about human goods, distinct from science and from art. Intuitive reason grasps the first principles from which demonstration starts, and philosophic wisdom is intuitive reason combined with scientific knowledge of the highest things, as in Anaxagoras and Thales. Practical wisdom concerns particulars and needs experience, which is why the young can be mathematicians but not practically wise. Aristotle relates it to political wisdom, household management and legislation, and ends by saying it grasps the ultimate particular by a kind of perception.","ideas":["Temperance preserves practical wisdom because pleasure and pain corrupt judgements about action","Philosophic wisdom is ranked above politics because man is not the best thing in the world","Practical wisdom needs knowledge of particulars, gained by experience","Political and practical wisdom are the same state but differ in essence"],"terms":[{"t":"phronēsis (practical wisdom)","d":"A reasoned and true state of capacity to act with regard to human goods."},{"t":"nous (intuitive reason)","d":"The state that grasps first principles, which cannot be demonstrated."},{"t":"sophia (philosophic wisdom)","d":"Intuitive reason combined with scientific knowledge of the things highest by nature."}]},{"i":307,"j":321,"loc":"Book VI (3 of 3)","title":"Deliberation, cleverness and the unity of virtue","summary":"Aristotle defines excellence in deliberation as correctness of thinking about what conduces to a good end, by the right means and at the right time. He distinguishes understanding, which only judges, and judgement, the right discrimination of the equitable, noting that all these faculties deal with particulars. He then asks what use practical and philosophic wisdom are, and answers that both are worth choosing in themselves, that philosophic wisdom produces happiness, and that practical wisdom makes us take the right means while virtue sets the mark. Cleverness without virtue is mere smartness, and natural virtue becomes virtue in the strict sense only with practical wisdom, so Socrates was partly right; the section ends by denying that practical wisdom rules over philosophic wisdom.","ideas":["Good deliberation is correctness relative to the true end","Virtue sets the end, practical wisdom the means","One cannot be practically wise without being good, nor fully good without practical wisdom","With practical wisdom all the virtues are present together"],"terms":[{"t":"euboulia (excellence in deliberation)","d":"Correctness of deliberation regarding what conduces to the end grasped by practical wisdom."},{"t":"deinotēs (cleverness)","d":"The ability to hit whatever mark one sets, laudable only when the mark is noble."},{"t":"natural virtue","d":"Inborn dispositions to the virtues, which can be harmful without reason."}]},{"i":322,"j":339,"loc":"Book VII (1 of 4)","title":"Incontinence: the puzzles and acting against knowledge","summary":"Book VII opens by naming three states to avoid, vice, incontinence and brutishness, opposed by virtue, continence and a heroic, godlike virtue. Aristotle sets out the common opinions about continence and incontinence and the puzzles they raise, above all Socrates' claim that no one acts against what he judges best. He answers by distinguishing knowledge that is had but not used, the universal and particular premisses, and the state of men asleep, mad or drunk, whose speech means no more than an actor's. Appetite can push a man against a universal rule while he holds a particular opinion, so in a sense Socrates' position results: what is overcome is perceptual knowledge, not knowledge proper.","ideas":["Method: set out appearances, raise difficulties, then save the most authoritative opinions","Having knowledge without using it explains incontinent action","The practical syllogism: a universal premiss and a particular premiss lead to action","Lower animals are not incontinent because they lack universal judgement"],"terms":[{"t":"akrasia (incontinence)","d":"Acting against one's own judgement under the influence of passion."},{"t":"enkrateia (continence)","d":"Abiding by one's rational principle against bad appetites."},{"t":"thēriotēs (brutishness)","d":"A state beyond human vice, found rarely and chiefly from disease, deformity or savagery."}]},{"i":340,"j":358,"loc":"Book VII (2 of 4)","title":"Objects of incontinence; brutish and morbid states","summary":"Aristotle argues that incontinence without qualification concerns the same bodily pleasures as self-indulgence; incontinence about honour, gain or anger is so called only by resemblance. He describes brutish and morbid states, from cannibalism and Phalaris to habits like nail-biting, and places them beyond the limits of vice. Incontinence in anger is less disgraceful than incontinence in appetite, since anger half-hears reason, is more natural, is open rather than guileful, and acts with pain rather than pleasure. He then separates self-indulgence, which chooses excess, from incontinence and softness, and opposes softness to endurance.","ideas":["Anger mishears the argument like hasty servants or barking dogs","Brutishness is less evil than vice but more alarming","The self-indulgent man is worse than the incontinent because he acts by choice","Continence conquers while endurance only resists"],"terms":[{"t":"malakia (softness)","d":"Failing to resist pains that most people resist successfully."},{"t":"karteria (endurance)","d":"Resisting pains, as opposed to continence, which conquers pleasures."}]},{"i":359,"j":374,"loc":"Book VII (3 of 4)","title":"Kinds of incontinence and its difference from vice","summary":"Aristotle divides incontinence into impetuosity, which acts without deliberating, and weakness, which deliberates but does not stand by its conclusion. The self-indulgent man stands by his choice and is incurable, while the incontinent repents and is curable, like epilepsy compared with a lasting disease. Incontinence is not vice, since it is contrary to choice and keeps the first principle intact. He distinguishes the continent from the strong-headed, explains Neoptolemus as yielding to a noble pleasure, and concludes that a practically wise man cannot be incontinent, though a clever one can; the incontinent is like a city with good laws it does not use.","ideas":["Vice is unaware of itself; incontinence is not","Virtue preserves the first principle of action, vice destroys it","The incontinent man is half-wicked but not a criminal","Incontinence from habit is easier to cure than incontinence by nature"],"terms":[{"t":"propeteia (impetuosity)","d":"The form of incontinence in which emotion leads a man before he deliberates."},{"t":"astheneia (weakness)","d":"The form of incontinence in which a man deliberates but fails to abide by his decision."}]},{"i":375,"j":392,"loc":"Book VII (4 of 4)","title":"First discussion of pleasure","summary":"Aristotle states that the political philosopher must study pleasure and pain, and lists three views: that no pleasure is good, that most are bad, and that pleasure cannot be the chief good. He answers that pleasure is not a perceptible process but unimpeded activity of the natural state, and that only foreign pleasures hinder thought. Against Speusippus he argues that since pain is bad, pleasure is a good, and that the chief good may be some pleasure, since happiness is unimpeded activity. He explains why bodily pleasures seem most choiceworthy, because they cure pain and are intense, and ends noting that God enjoys a single, simple pleasure.","ideas":["Pleasure is activity, not a process toward a natural state","The happy man needs bodily and external goods so as not to be impeded","One should explain why false views seem true","Pleasure lies more in rest than in movement"],"terms":[{"t":"hēdonē (pleasure)","d":"Here defined as unimpeded activity of the natural state."},{"t":"energeia (activity)","d":"The exercise of a faculty, which Aristotle contrasts with a process of becoming."}]},{"i":393,"j":405,"loc":"Book VIII (1 of 4)","title":"Why friendship matters and its three kinds","summary":"Book VIII begins with friendship as a virtue or something implying virtue, necessary for rich and poor, young and old, and the bond that holds states together. After setting aside physical theories of like and unlike, Aristotle identifies three objects of love, the good, the pleasant and the useful, and so three kinds of friendship, each requiring mutual, recognized goodwill. Friendships of utility and pleasure are incidental and easily dissolved, typical of the old and the young respectively. Perfect friendship is between good men alike in virtue, who wish each other well for each other's sake; it is lasting, rare, takes time, and is proof against slander.","ideas":["When men are friends they have no need of justice","Goodwill becomes friendship only when reciprocal and recognized","Utility and pleasure friendships love the other for what he provides","Only the good can be friends for each other's own sake"],"terms":[{"t":"philia (friendship)","d":"Mutual and recognized goodwill grounded in the good, the pleasant or the useful."},{"t":"eunoia (goodwill)","d":"Wishing good to another for his sake, which alone is not yet friendship."}]},{"i":406,"j":417,"loc":"Book VIII (2 of 4)","title":"Living together, number of friends, unequal friendships","summary":"Aristotle distinguishes friendship as a state from its activity, which is above all living together; distance interrupts the activity and old or sour people make friends less easily. Love is a feeling but friendship a state of character involving choice. One cannot have perfect friendship with many people, and men in power tend to keep separate friends for use and for pleasure. He then turns to friendships between unequals, such as father and son or ruler and subject, where love must be proportional to merit, and notes that when the gap is very great, as with gods, friendship ceases. Friendship lies more in loving than in being loved, as mothers show.","ideas":["Nothing is so characteristic of friends as living together","Friendship is said to be equality","In justice proportion is primary; in friendship quantitative equality is","Loving is the characteristic virtue of friends"],"terms":[{"t":"philēsis (love)","d":"A feeling that can be directed even at lifeless things, unlike friendship."}]},{"i":418,"j":428,"loc":"Book VIII (3 of 4)","title":"Friendship, justice, constitutions and family","summary":"Aristotle argues that friendship and justice extend over the same persons and communities, and that all communities are parts of the political community, which aims at advantage for the whole of life. He sets out three constitutions, monarchy, aristocracy and timocracy, with their deviations, tyranny, oligarchy and democracy, and finds their patterns in household relations. Friendship exists in each constitution as far as justice does, and hardly at all in tyranny; there is none with a slave as slave, but there can be with him as a man. He then treats the friendship of kin, deriving it from parental love, and of man and wife, grounded in nature, utility, pleasure and possibly virtue.","ideas":["What friends have is common property","Injustice is worse the closer the friendship","Father to son resembles kingship; husband to wife aristocracy; brothers timocracy","Parents love children as other selves"],"terms":[{"t":"koinōnia (community)","d":"An association of persons in which both justice and friendship are found."},{"t":"timocracy","d":"Constitution based on a property qualification, which most people call polity."}]},{"i":429,"j":435,"loc":"Book VIII (4 of 4)","title":"Complaints and repayment between friends","summary":"Complaints arise chiefly in friendships of utility, since each party wants the better bargain. Aristotle distinguishes a legal type of utility friendship on fixed terms from a moral type that gives as to a friend but expects a return, and says one should repay if possible. In utility friendships the benefit to the receiver is the measure; in virtue friendships the purpose of the giver is. In unequal friendships the superior should receive more honour and the inferior more gain. Debts to gods and parents can never be fully repaid, which is why a son may not disown his father though a father may disown a son.","ideas":["Men wish for what is noble but choose what is advantageous","Consider at the outset on what terms a benefit is offered","Honour is the prize of virtue; gain the aid to inferiority","Friendship asks a man to do what he can"],"terms":[]},{"i":436,"j":448,"loc":"Book IX (1 of 4)","title":"Fixing returns, rival claims, ending friendships","summary":"Book IX continues with proportion between dissimilar friends: disputes arise when, for example, a lover seeks pleasure and the beloved utility. Aristotle considers who should fix the worth of a service, citing Protagoras, and holds that where there is no contract the receiver should fix it, while teachers of philosophy, like gods and parents, can only be repaid as far as possible. Conflicting claims of father, friend, benefactor and others cannot be settled precisely; generally debts should be paid and each class given what is appropriate. He then asks when friendships may be broken off: utility and pleasure friendships end with their grounds, a friend gone incurably bad may be given up, and past intimacy still deserves some regard.","ideas":["Purpose is the measure of return in friendships of virtue","One should ransom one's father even before oneself","Discussions of actions are only as definite as their subject-matter","Deceiving a friend about one's motive is worse than counterfeiting money"],"terms":[]},{"i":449,"j":461,"loc":"Book IX (2 of 4)","title":"Friendship toward oneself, goodwill, unanimity, benefactors","summary":"Aristotle derives the marks of friendship from a good man's relation to himself: he wishes himself good, wishes to live, enjoys his own company and is at one with himself, and his friend is another self. Bad men are at variance with themselves, flee their own company and are full of repentance. Goodwill is a beginning of friendship, like the pleasure of the eye in love, and unanimity is political friendship about matters of action, found among the good. Finally he explains why benefactors love those they benefit more than the reverse: the benefited is their handiwork, in which their own existence is realized in activity.","ideas":["A friend is another self","The thinking element seems to be the man himself","Goodwill is inactive friendship","Craftsmen and poets love their works as benefactors love those they help"],"terms":[{"t":"homonoia (unanimity)","d":"Agreement about practical matters of consequence, described as political friendship."}]},{"i":462,"j":466,"loc":"Book IX (3 of 4)","title":"Self-love rightly and wrongly understood","summary":"Aristotle sets out the conflict between those who blame self-lovers and those who say a man is his own best friend and should love himself most. He resolves it by distinguishing two senses: the common, blameworthy self-love takes more than its share of wealth, honours and bodily pleasures, while the good man assigns himself what is noble and gratifies his reason, which is most truly himself. Such a man will give up wealth, honour, office and even his life for friends and country, thereby taking the greater share of nobility. So the good man should be a lover of self, but the wicked should not.","ideas":["Reason is the most authoritative element and most truly the man","Dying for others is choosing a great prize","It may be nobler to cause a friend's action than to act oneself"],"terms":[{"t":"philautos (lover of self)","d":"A term of reproach in its common use, but in its true sense a description of the good man."}]},{"i":467,"j":481,"loc":"Book IX (4 of 4)","title":"Does the happy man need friends?","summary":"Against the view that the self-sufficient happy man needs no friends, Aristotle argues that friends are the greatest external good, that man is a political creature, and that the good man needs people to benefit. Since happiness is activity and we observe friends' actions better than our own, the happy man needs virtuous friends; activity with others is also more continuous. A longer argument holds that perceiving one's own existence is pleasant, and the friend's existence is almost as desirable, realized by living together and sharing discussion and thought. He discusses how many friends one should have, the role of friends in good and bad fortune, and closes with living together as the heart of friendship.","ideas":["To perceive that we perceive is to perceive that we exist","Human living together means sharing talk and thought, not grazing together","Friends should be as many as one can live with; great friendship is with few","Summon friends readily to good fortune, hesitantly to bad"],"terms":[{"t":"autarkeia (self-sufficiency)","d":"The completeness of the happy life, which Aristotle argues still includes friends."}]},{"i":482,"j":491,"loc":"Book X (1 of 4)","title":"Pleasure: Eudoxus and the opposing views","summary":"Book X returns to pleasure, which steers education and bears on virtue; Aristotle insists true arguments are needed because false ones are discredited by conduct. He reports Eudoxus' arguments that pleasure is the good, since all things aim at it, avoid its contrary, and choose it for itself, and notes that his character lent them credit. Using Plato's argument, he says pleasure added to another good shows it is only one good among others. He then criticizes the opposing arguments: that pleasure is indeterminate, a movement or coming into being, or a replenishment, and that disgraceful pleasures show it bad. He concludes that pleasure is not the good, not all pleasure is desirable, and some pleasures are desirable in themselves.","ideas":["What all believe is likely true","Pleasure cannot be quick or slow, so it is not a movement","Many pleasures, such as learning, presuppose no pain","We would choose seeing, knowing and virtue even without pleasure"],"terms":[]},{"i":492,"j":501,"loc":"Book X (2 of 4)","title":"What pleasure is: completion of activity","summary":"Aristotle argues that pleasure, like seeing, is complete at every moment and so is not a movement, which is complete only in its whole time, as the building of a temple shows. Pleasure arises when a sense or thought in good condition acts on its finest object, and it completes the activity as a supervening end, like the bloom of youth. Pleasure is not continuous because activity is not, and life and pleasure seem bound together. Pleasures differ in kind as activities do; proper pleasures intensify an activity and alien ones impede it, as flute-music distracts an arguer. The pleasures proper to man are those that complete the activities of the good man, who is the measure.","ideas":["Movement is incomplete in its parts; pleasure is a whole at any moment","Pleasure completes activity","Alien pleasures act much like proper pains","What appears pleasant to the good man is truly pleasant"],"terms":[{"t":"kinēsis (movement)","d":"A change that takes time and is complete only on reaching its end."}]},{"i":502,"j":513,"loc":"Book X (3 of 4)","title":"Happiness, amusement and the contemplative life","summary":"Aristotle recalls that happiness is an activity desirable in itself, and rejects amusement as its substance: we amuse ourselves in order to work, as Anacharsis says. Happiness is activity according to the highest virtue, that of reason, and this activity is contemplative: the best, most continuous, pleasantest, most self-sufficient and leisured. Such a life is divine rather than merely human, yet we should strive to live by the best element in us, which is each man's self. The life of moral virtue is happy in a secondary way and needs more external goods. The gods' activity must be contemplation, animals have no share in happiness, and the philosopher, needing only moderate means, is dearest to the gods and happiest.","ideas":["Relaxation is for the sake of activity","We must make ourselves immortal so far as we can","Moral virtue belongs to our composite nature; reason is a thing apart","Happiness extends just so far as contemplation does"],"terms":[{"t":"theōria (contemplation)","d":"The activity of reason, identified as perfect happiness."},{"t":"eudaimonia (happiness)","d":"Activity in accordance with virtue, and most fully with the highest virtue."},{"t":"scholē (leisure)","d":"The freedom from business on which happiness is thought to depend."}]},{"i":514,"j":525,"loc":"Book X (4 of 4)","title":"From ethics to legislation","summary":"Aristotle says knowing about virtue is not enough; we must have and use it, but arguments move only the generous, not the many who obey fear. Since nature is not in our power and teaching needs soil prepared by habit, upbringing and adult life must be governed by law, which has compulsive force while proceeding from practical wisdom. Where the city neglects this, as almost everywhere but Sparta, each man should help his children and friends, and he does this better by acquiring legislative skill, which requires universal knowledge as well as experience. Sophists who teach politics do not understand it, and collections of laws help only the experienced, so he proposes to study constitutions to complete the philosophy of human nature.","ideas":["Character must be cultivated like earth before the seed of argument","Law can compel without being resented as a man is","Private education can fit each case more precisely","The study turns to constitutions, leading toward the Politics"],"terms":[{"t":"ethos (habituation)","d":"The training in noble joy and hatred that must precede effective argument."},{"t":"nomothetikē (legislative science)","d":"The skill of making laws, which Aristotle says one should acquire to make others better."}]}]});